## Solution

**Problem.** Evaluate $I = \int_0^\pi e^{ix}\sqrt{1-e^{2ix}}\,dx$.

### Step 1 — Substitution $z = e^{ix}$

Set $z = e^{ix}$, so $dz = i\,e^{ix}\,dx = iz\,dx$, i.e. $dx = \frac{dz}{iz}$. As $x$ runs from $0$ to $\pi$, $z$ traces the **upper semicircle** $\gamma$ from $1$ to $-1$ (counterclockwise). Then

$$
I = \int_\gamma z\,\sqrt{1-z^2}\;\frac{dz}{iz} = \frac{1}{i}\int_\gamma \sqrt{1-z^2}\;dz.
$$

### Step 2 — Branch of the square root

Take the **principal branch** of $\sqrt{\,\cdot\,}$ (cut on $(-\infty,0]$). The induced cut of $\sqrt{1-z^2}$ in the $z$-plane is the set where $1-z^2\in(-\infty,0]$, i.e. $z\in(-\infty,-1]\cup[1,\infty)$ on the real axis.

**Sanity check along $\gamma$.** For $x\in(0,\pi)$,
$$
1-e^{2ix}=2\sin^2 x - i\,(2\sin x\cos x),
$$
so $\operatorname{Re}(1-e^{2ix})=2\sin^2 x>0$: the image of $1-z^2$ along $\gamma$ stays in the **open right half-plane**, never touching the cut. Hence $\sqrt{1-z^2}$ is single-valued and continuous on $\gamma$.

### Step 3 — Deform the contour to the real segment

Let $C$ be the closed contour = $\gamma$ (upper semicircle $1\to -1$) $\cup$ the segment $[-1,1]$ ($-1\to 1$). This bounds the **upper half-disk**, which is disjoint from the cuts $(-\infty,-1]\cup[1,\infty)$. The function $\sqrt{1-z^2}$ is analytic in the interior and continuous up to the boundary; near the endpoints $z=\pm1$ it vanishes like $O(|z\mp1|^{1/2})$, so small indentations contribute $O(\varepsilon^{3/2})\to0$. By Cauchy's theorem,

$$
\int_\gamma \sqrt{1-z^2}\,dz \;+\; \int_{-1}^{1}\sqrt{1-z^2}\,dz \;=\; 0,
\qquad\text{i.e.}\qquad
\int_\gamma \sqrt{1-z^2}\,dz = -\int_{-1}^{1}\sqrt{1-z^2}\,dz.
$$

### Step 4 — Evaluate the real integral

For $z\in[-1,1]$, $1-z^2\ge 0$, so $\sqrt{1-z^2}$ is the usual non-negative real function. Thus

$$
\int_{-1}^{1}\sqrt{1-z^2}\,dz = \frac{\pi}{2}
$$

(area of the upper semicircle of radius $1$).

### Step 5 — Assemble

$$
I = \frac{1}{i}\!\left(-\frac{\pi}{2}\right) = -\frac{\pi}{2i} = \frac{\pi\, i}{2}.
$$

### Verification (Beta-function route)

Using $1-e^{2ix}=-2i\,e^{ix}\sin x$ and the principal branch, for $x\in(0,\pi)$:

$$
\sqrt{1-e^{2ix}}=\sqrt{2\sin x}\;e^{i(x/2-\pi/4)},\qquad
e^{ix}\sqrt{1-e^{2ix}}=\sqrt{2\sin x}\;e^{i(3x/2-\pi/4)}.
$$

Hence

$$
I=\sqrt{2}\,e^{-i\pi/4}\int_0^\pi \sin^{1/2}x\;e^{i\,3x/2}\,dx.
$$

The standard formula $\displaystyle\int_0^\pi \sin^{\nu-1}x\,e^{i\mu x}\,dx=\frac{\pi\,e^{i\mu\pi/2}\,\Gamma(\nu)}{2^{\nu-1}\,\Gamma\!\bigl(\frac{\nu+\mu+1}{2}\bigr)\,\Gamma\!\bigl(\frac{\nu-\mu+1}{2}\bigr)}$ with $\nu=\tfrac32,\;\mu=\tfrac32$ gives

$$
\int_0^\pi \sin^{1/2}x\;e^{i3x/2}\,dx
=\frac{\pi\,e^{i3\pi/4}\,\Gamma(3/2)}{\sqrt{2}\;\Gamma(2)\,\Gamma(1/2)}
=\frac{\pi\,e^{i3\pi/4}\cdot\frac{\sqrt\pi}{2}}{\sqrt{2}\cdot1\cdot\sqrt\pi}
=\frac{\pi\,e^{i3\pi/4}}{2\sqrt{2}}.
$$

Therefore

$$
I=\sqrt{2}\,e^{-i\pi/4}\cdot\frac{\pi\,e^{i3\pi/4}}{2\sqrt{2}}
=\frac{\pi}{2}\,e^{i\pi/2}
=\frac{\pi\,i}{2}.
$$

Both methods agree.

$$
\boxed{\displaystyle\int_0^\pi e^{ix}\sqrt{1-e^{2ix}}\,dx \;=\; \frac{\pi\, i}{2}}
$$

